IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ea-cpc-interface`
IMPORT `ea-types`

-- Evidence Act 1893, Part 3 "Witnesses": sections 120 to 136.
-- Source: source/EA1893.txt lines 3503 to 3966.
--
-- The questions this module answers: WHO MAY TESTIFY AND WHO CAN BE COMPELLED (120 to 122, 135, 136),
-- WHAT A WITNESS CANNOT BE COMPELLED TO DISCLOSE OR PRODUCE (the privileges and protections of 123 to
-- 133), and WHAT A WITNESS MAY NOT REFUSE ON THE GROUND OF SELF-INCRIMINATION (134).  Section 122(4)
-- to (8), on what the accused may be asked in cross-examination, is the answer the Criminal Procedure
-- Code's section 291(5)(a) needs: see ea-conclusions.l4.

§ `120 to 121. Who may testify`

-- 120: all persons are competent to testify unless the court considers that they are prevented from
-- understanding the questions put to them, or from giving rational answers to them, by tender years,
-- extreme old age, disease (of body or mind) or any other cause of the same kind.  The Explanation: a
-- lunatic is not incompetent unless prevented by lunacy from understanding and answering.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 120 and Explanation"
GIVEN c IS A `Competence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is competent to testify under section 120` IF
    NOT c's `the court considers that the person is prevented from understanding the questions put or from giving rational answers by tender years, extreme old age, disease of body or mind, or another cause of the same kind`

-- 121: a witness unable to speak may give evidence in any other manner in which the witness can make it
-- intelligible, as by writing or signs, but written and made in open court; evidence so given is deemed
-- to be oral evidence.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 121(1) and (2)"
GIVEN c IS A `Competence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the evidence of the witness who is unable to speak is deemed oral evidence` IF
    c's `the witness is unable to speak and gives evidence in another manner in which the witness can make it intelligible, in open court`

§ `122(1) to (3). Parties and spouses; the accused as a witness`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 122(1)"
GIVEN c IS A `Competence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the party or the spouse of a party is a competent witness in civil proceedings` IF
        c's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `civil proceedings`
    AND (   c's `the person is a party to the suit`
         OR c's `the person is the husband or wife of a party to the suit`)

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 122(2)"
GIVEN c IS A `Competence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the spouse of the accused is a competent witness in criminal proceedings` IF
        c's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
    AND c's `the person is the husband or wife of a person under criminal trial`

-- 122(3): in any criminal proceedings, the accused is competent to give evidence on behalf of himself or
-- herself or any person jointly charged, but is not compellable to do so.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 122(3)"
GIVEN c IS A `Competence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused is competent to give evidence under section 122(3)` IF
        c's `the kind of proceedings` EQUALS `criminal proceedings`
    AND c's `the person is the accused`
    AND c's `the evidence is on behalf of the accused or a person jointly charged with the accused`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 122(3)"
`the accused is compellable to give evidence under section 122(3)` MEANS FALSE

§ `122(4) to (8). Questions to the accused in cross-examination`

-- 122(4): where the accused gives evidence, then, subject to the section and to section 56, the accused
-- must not in cross-examination be asked, and if asked is not required to answer, any question tending to
-- reveal (a) that the accused has committed, or been charged with, convicted or acquitted of, an offence
-- other than the offence charged, or (b) that the accused is generally or in a particular respect a person
-- of bad disposition or reputation.
-- 122(5): (4) does not apply if evidence of the fact is admissible for proving the commission of the offence
-- charged by virtue of section 14 or 15 of the Act, section 265 or 266 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 or
-- another written law.  The Code's two sections reach this module as `Code Conclusions`.
-- 122(6): where two or more persons are jointly charged, and any of the accused gives evidence, (4) does not
-- apply to a question revealing a fact if evidence of it is admissible to show another of the accused not guilty.
-- 122(7): (4) does not apply if the accused (or the accused's advocate) has asked a prosecution witness, or a
-- witness for a person jointly charged, a question about the witness's conduct or offences, and the court is of
-- the opinion that its main purpose was to raise an issue as to the witness's credibility, but the court is not
-- to permit such a question to be put to the accused unless it is of the opinion that the question is relevant
-- to the accused's credibility as a witness.
-- 122(8): (4) does not apply where the accused has given evidence against a person jointly charged.
-- 56(4): where a party is entitled under section 56 to cross-examine the accused to establish bad disposition or
-- reputation, 122(4) does not apply to that cross-examination.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 122(4) to (8) and s 56(4)"
GIVEN a IS AN `Accused Cross-examination Facts`, c IS A `Code Conclusions`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `section 122(4) forbids the question to be put to the accused` IF
        a's `the accused gives evidence in the criminal proceedings`
    AND (   a's `the question tends to reveal that the accused has committed, or has been charged with or convicted or acquitted of, an offence other than the offence charged`
         OR a's `the question tends to reveal that the accused is generally or in a particular respect a person of bad disposition or reputation`)
    AND NOT a's `evidence of the fact is admissible for proving the commission of the offence charged by virtue of section 14 or 15 of the Evidence Act 1893 or another written law`
    AND NOT c's `evidence of the fact is admissible by virtue of section 265 or 266 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010`
    AND NOT a's `2 or more persons are jointly charged and the evidence of the fact is admissible for the purpose of showing another of the accused to be not guilty`
    AND NOT (   a's `the accused personally or by an advocate asked a witness for the prosecution or for a person jointly charged a question concerning the witness's conduct on an occasion or whether the witness has committed, or been charged with, convicted or acquitted of, an offence`
            AND a's `the court is of the opinion that the main purpose of that question was to raise an issue as to the witness's credibility`
            AND a's `the court is of the opinion that the question is relevant to the accused's credibility as a witness`)
    AND NOT a's `the accused has given evidence against a person jointly charged in the same proceedings`
    AND NOT a's `the party cross-examining is entitled under section 56 to establish that the accused is a person of bad disposition or reputation`

§ `123 to 133. Privileges and protections from compulsion`

-- 123: no Judge, and (except on the special order of the General Division of the High Court) no Magistrate, may be
-- compelled to answer any question as to his or her own conduct in court as such, or as to anything which came
-- to his or her knowledge in court as such; but may be examined as to other matters that occurred in his or her
-- presence while so acting.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 123"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Judge or Magistrate may be compelled to answer under section 123` IF
        NOT p's `the question is as to the witness's own conduct in court as a Judge or Magistrate, or as to anything that came to the witness's knowledge in court as such`
    OR  (   NOT p's `the witness is a Judge`
        AND p's `the witness is a Magistrate and the General Division of the High Court has made a special order`)

-- 124(1): no person who is or has been married may be compelled to disclose any communication made during the
-- marriage by the person married to him or her, nor be permitted to disclose it unless the maker or the maker's
-- representative in interest consents, except in suits between married persons or proceedings in which one married
-- person is prosecuted for any crime committed against the other.  124(2): nothing in the section protects from
-- disclosure a communication that is relevant in criminal proceedings in respect of a specified offence.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 124(1) and (2)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`, `the communication is relevant in criminal proceedings in respect of a specified offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the communication during marriage is protected from disclosure under section 124` IF
        p's `the communication was made to the witness during marriage by a person to whom the witness is or has been married`
    AND NOT p's `the person who made it, or that person's representative in interest, consents to disclosure`
    AND NOT p's `the proceedings are a suit between married persons or proceedings in which one married person is prosecuted for a crime committed against the other`
    AND NOT `the communication is relevant in criminal proceedings in respect of a specified offence`

-- 124(3) "specified offence": (a) a child abuse offence committed against a person below 16; (b) a sexual offence, or
-- an offence under Chapter 16 of the Penal Code 1871 (other than a sexual offence), committed against a person
-- below 16; (c) a sexual offence or Chapter 16 offence committed against a domestic worker by the employer, a member
-- of the employer's household or an employment agent; (d) a sexual offence or Chapter 16 offence committed against a
-- vulnerable person by a person who has custody, charge or care of that person; (e) an abetment of, conspiracy to
-- commit or attempt to commit an offence in (a) to (d).  "Domestic worker", "employer", "employment agent", "member of
-- the employer's household" and "vulnerable person" take their meaning from sections 73(4) and 74A(5) of the Penal Code
-- 1871, which are not encoded: whether a person is one is a fact.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 124(3) 'specified offence' (a) to (e)"
GIVEN o IS A `Specified Offence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence is a specified offence` IF
        (   o's `the offence is a child abuse offence`
        AND o's `the offence was committed against a person below 16 years of age`)
    OR  (   (   o's `the offence is a sexual offence`
             OR o's `the offence is an offence under Chapter 16 of the Penal Code 1871 other than a sexual offence`)
        AND (   o's `the offence was committed against a person below 16 years of age`
             OR o's `the offence was committed against a domestic worker by an employer of the domestic worker, a member of the employer's household or an employment agent of the domestic worker`
             OR o's `the offence was committed against a vulnerable person by a person who has the custody, charge or care of the vulnerable person`))
    OR  o's `the offence is an abetment of, a conspiracy to commit or an attempt to commit an offence mentioned in paragraphs (a) to (d) of the definition`

-- 125: no one may produce unpublished official records relating to affairs of State, or give evidence derived from
-- them, except with the permission of the officer at the head of the Department concerned, who may give or withhold it
-- as he or she thinks fit, subject to the control of the Minister.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 125"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the evidence of unpublished official records may be given under section 125` IF
        NOT p's `the evidence is of unpublished official records relating to affairs of State, or evidence derived from them`
    OR  p's `the officer at the head of the Department concerned has given permission`

-- 126(1) and (2): a public officer, or a member, officer, employee or seconded person of an organisation specified in
-- the Schedule to the Official Secrets Act 1935, may not be compelled to disclose communications made in official
-- confidence when he or she considers that the public interest would suffer by the disclosure.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 126(1) and (2)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the official may not be compelled to disclose the communication under section 126` IF
        p's `the witness is a public officer, or a member, officer or employee of, or a person seconded to, an organisation specified in the Schedule to the Official Secrets Act 1935`
    AND p's `the communication was made to the witness in official confidence`
    AND p's `the witness considers that the public interest would suffer by the disclosure`

-- 127(1) and (2): no Magistrate or police officer may be compelled to say from where he or she got any information as to
-- the commission of any offence; no revenue officer may be compelled to say so as to any information as to an offence
-- against the public revenue or the excise laws.  This is the informer privilege and bears directly on a first
-- information report (CPC ss 14 and 15): the fact that an offence was reported and what the report said are
-- not within it; only the source of the information is.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 127(1) and (2)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the officer may not be compelled to say where the information was got under section 127` IF
        p's `the witness is a Magistrate or police officer, or a revenue officer asked about an offence against the public revenue or the excise laws`
    AND p's `the question is from where the witness got information as to the commission of an offence`

-- 128(1): no advocate or solicitor is at any time permitted, unless with the client's express consent, to disclose any
-- communication made to him or her in the course and for the purpose of his or her employment by or on behalf of the
-- client, to state the contents or condition of any document with which he or she became acquainted in the course and
-- for the purpose of the employment, or to disclose any advice given to the client in the course and for the purpose of
-- the employment.  128(2): nothing protects (a) a communication made in furtherance of any illegal purpose, or (b) any
-- fact observed in the course of the employment showing that any crime or fraud has been committed since the
-- commencement of the employment.  128(3): immaterial whether the advocate's attention was directed to the fact by the
-- client.  The obligation continues after the employment has ceased (Explanation).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 128(1), (2) and (3)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the advocate or solicitor is not permitted to disclose under section 128` IF
        p's `the kind of privilege` EQUALS `professional communications with an advocate or solicitor (section 128)`
    AND (   p's `the communication was made to the advocate or solicitor, or legal counsel, in the course and for the purpose of the employment`
         OR p's `the document became known to the advocate or solicitor, or legal counsel, in the course and for the purpose of the employment`
         OR p's `the advice was given to the client, or entity, in the course and for the purpose of the employment`)
    AND NOT p's `the client, or entity, has given express consent`
    AND NOT p's `the communication was made in furtherance of an illegal purpose`
    AND NOT p's `the fact was observed in the course of the employment and shows that a crime or fraud has been committed since the commencement of the employment`

-- 128A(1) and (2): the same for a legal counsel in an entity, with the entity's express consent, and with two more
-- exceptions: (c) a communication to the legal counsel not made for the purpose of seeking legal advice, and (d) a
-- document the legal counsel was made acquainted with otherwise than in the course of and for the purpose of seeking
-- legal advice.  128A(4) and (5) extend the entity to related corporations and public agencies; those extensions
-- decide who "the entity" is and are not conditions.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 128A(1) to (3)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the legal counsel in an entity is not permitted to disclose under section 128A` IF
        p's `the kind of privilege` EQUALS `communications with legal counsel in an entity (section 128A)`
    AND (   p's `the communication was made to the advocate or solicitor, or legal counsel, in the course and for the purpose of the employment`
         OR p's `the document became known to the advocate or solicitor, or legal counsel, in the course and for the purpose of the employment`
         OR p's `the advice was given to the client, or entity, in the course and for the purpose of the employment`)
    AND NOT p's `the client, or entity, has given express consent`
    AND NOT p's `the communication was made in furtherance of an illegal purpose`
    AND NOT p's `the fact was observed in the course of the employment and shows that a crime or fraud has been committed since the commencement of the employment`
    AND NOT p's `the communication to the legal counsel was not made for the purpose of seeking legal advice`
    AND NOT p's `the document became known to the legal counsel otherwise than in the course of and for the purpose of seeking legal advice`

-- 129: sections 128 and 128A apply to interpreters and other persons who work under the supervision of legal professional
-- advisers.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 129"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `sections 128 and 128A apply to the witness under section 129` IF
    p's `the witness is an interpreter or other person working under the supervision of a legal professional adviser`

-- 130: (1) a party who gives evidence is not thereby deemed to consent to disclosure under 128 or 128A; (2) a party who
-- calls an advocate or solicitor as a witness is deemed to consent to disclosure under 128 only if the party questions
-- the advocate on matters which but for the question the advocate would not be at liberty to disclose; (3) the same for
-- a legal counsel and 128A.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 130(1), (2) and (3)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the party is deemed to have consented to the disclosure under section 130` IF
    p's `the party called the advocate or solicitor, or legal counsel, as a witness and questioned the witness on matters that but for the question the witness would not be at liberty to disclose`

-- 131: no one may be compelled to disclose to the court any confidential communication between him or her and his or
-- her legal professional adviser unless he or she offers himself or herself as a witness, in which case he or she may
-- be compelled to disclose such communications as appear to the court necessary to be known to explain the evidence
-- given, but no others.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 131(1)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person may be compelled to disclose the confidential communication under section 131` IF
        p's `the communication was a confidential communication between the person and the person's legal professional adviser`
    AND p's `the person offers himself or herself as a witness`
    AND p's `the communication appears to the court necessary to be known in order to explain evidence the person has given`

-- 132(1): a non-party witness may not be compelled to produce title deeds, documents by which the witness holds property
-- as pledgee or mortgagee, or documents tending to criminate the witness, unless the witness has agreed in writing to
-- produce them.  132(2): a witness who is a party is not bound to produce a document not relevant or material to the case
-- of the party requiring its production.  132(3): no bank may be compelled to produce its books in a proceeding to which
-- it is not a party except as provided by section 174.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 132(1)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the non-party witness may not be compelled to produce the document under section 132(1)` IF
        p's `the witness is not a party to the suit`
    AND p's `the document is the witness's title deed to property, or a document by virtue of which the witness holds property as pledgee or mortgagee, or a document the production of which might tend to criminate the witness`
    AND NOT p's `the witness has agreed in writing to produce it with the person seeking production or some person through whom the witness claims`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 132(2)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the party witness is not bound to produce the document under section 132(2)` IF
    p's `the witness is a party to the suit and the document is not relevant or material to the case of the party requiring its production`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 132(3)"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the bank may not be compelled to produce its books under section 132(3)` IF
    p's `the witness is a bank, the proceeding is one to which the bank is not a party, and the books of the bank are sought other than as provided by section 174`

-- 133: no one may be compelled to produce documents in his or her possession which any other person would be entitled to
-- refuse to produce if they were in that person's possession, except for the purpose of identification, unless that other
-- person consents; nor may anyone entitled to refuse to produce a document be compelled to give oral evidence of its
-- contents.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 133"
GIVEN p IS A `Privilege Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person may not be compelled to produce the document under section 133` IF
        p's `the document is in the witness's possession and another person would be entitled to refuse to produce it if it were in that person's possession`
    AND NOT p's `the document is produced for the purpose of identification`
    AND NOT p's `that other person consents to its production`

§ `134. A witness is not excused on the ground of self-incrimination`

-- 134(1): a witness is not excused from answering any question as to any matter relevant to the matter in issue in any
-- suit or civil or criminal proceeding on the ground that the answer will or may tend, directly or indirectly, to criminate
-- the witness, expose the witness to a penalty or forfeiture, or establish that the witness owes a debt or is subject to
-- a civil suit.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 134(1)"
GIVEN s IS A `Self-incrimination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the witness is not excused from answering under section 134(1)` IF
        s's `the question is as to a matter relevant to the matter in issue in a suit or in a civil or criminal proceeding`
    AND s's `the answer will or may tend, directly or indirectly, to criminate the witness, expose the witness to a penalty or forfeiture, or establish that the witness owes a debt or is subject to a civil suit`

-- 134(2): no answer which a witness is compelled by the court to give subjects the witness to any arrest or prosecution,
-- or may be proved against the witness in any criminal proceeding, except a prosecution for giving false evidence by that
-- answer.  134(3): before compelling the answer the court is to explain the purport of subsection (2).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 134(2)"
GIVEN s IS A `Self-incrimination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the compelled answer may be proved against the witness in a criminal proceeding` IF
        s's `the witness was compelled by the court to give the answer`
    AND s's `the proceeding is a prosecution for giving false evidence by that answer`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 134(3)"
GIVEN s IS A `Self-incrimination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the court has complied with section 134(3) before compelling the answer` IF
    s's `the court has explained to the witness the purport of section 134(2)`

-- 134(4): where the accused gives evidence in criminal proceedings (a) the accused is not entitled to refuse to answer a
-- question or produce a document or thing on the ground that to do so would tend to prove the commission of the offence
-- charged; and (b) except for a question, document or thing the court considers relevant solely or mainly to the accused's
-- credibility (not being a question asked by virtue of section 56), is not entitled to refuse on the ground that to do so
-- would tend to expose the accused to proceedings for some other offence or for the recovery of a penalty, or to expose the
-- accused's wife or husband to proceedings for an offence or for the recovery of a penalty.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 134(4)(a)"
GIVEN s IS A `Self-incrimination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused may not refuse on the ground that the answer would tend to prove the offence charged under section 134(4)(a)` IF
        s's `the witness is the accused giving evidence in criminal proceedings`
    AND s's `the answer would tend to prove the commission by the accused of the offence charged`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 134(4)(b)"
GIVEN s IS A `Self-incrimination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accused may not refuse on the ground of exposure to other proceedings under section 134(4)(b)` IF
        s's `the witness is the accused giving evidence in criminal proceedings`
    AND (   s's `the answer would tend to expose the accused to proceedings for some other offence or for the recovery of a penalty`
         OR s's `the answer would tend to expose the accused's wife or husband to proceedings for an offence or for the recovery of a penalty`)
    AND (   NOT s's `the question, document or thing is in the opinion of the court relevant solely or mainly to the accused's credibility as a witness`
         OR s's `the question is one asked by virtue of section 56`)

-- 134(5): where a person who is the wife or husband of the accused gives evidence in any criminal proceedings, the person
-- (a) is not entitled to refuse to answer a question or produce a document on the ground that to do so would tend to prove the
-- commission by the accused of the offence charged; and (b) except as to a question the court considers relevant solely or
-- mainly to that person's credibility, is not entitled to refuse on the ground that it would tend to expose that person to
-- proceedings as in (4)(b)(i).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 134(5)(a)"
GIVEN s IS A `Self-incrimination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the spouse of the accused may not refuse on the ground that the answer would tend to prove the offence charged under section 134(5)(a)` IF
        s's `the witness is the wife or husband of the accused giving evidence in criminal proceedings`
    AND s's `the answer would tend to prove the commission by the accused of the offence charged`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 134(5)(b)"
GIVEN s IS A `Self-incrimination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the spouse of the accused may not refuse on the ground of exposure to other proceedings under section 134(5)(b)` IF
        s's `the witness is the wife or husband of the accused giving evidence in criminal proceedings`
    AND s's `the answer would tend to expose the witness to proceedings as mentioned in section 134(4)(b)(i)`
    AND NOT s's `the question, document or thing is in the opinion of the court relevant solely or mainly to the accused's credibility as a witness`

-- 134(6): no answer which an accused or spouse is compelled to give under (4)(b) or (5)(b) shall expose the accused to
-- proceedings for some other offence or for the recovery of a penalty, or be proved against the accused in any such
-- proceedings, or expose the spouse to proceedings for an offence or for recovery of a penalty or be proved against the
-- spouse.  134(7): proceedings for the recovery of a penalty include civil proceedings.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 134(6) and (7)"
GIVEN s IS A `Self-incrimination Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the compelled answer is protected from use in other proceedings under section 134(6)` IF
        s's `the witness was compelled by the court to give the answer`
    AND (   `the accused may not refuse on the ground of exposure to other proceedings under section 134(4)(b)` s
         OR `the spouse of the accused may not refuse on the ground of exposure to other proceedings under section 134(5)(b)` s)

§ `135 and 136. Accomplices and number of witnesses`

-- 135(1): an accomplice is a competent witness against an accused person.  135(2): any rule of law or practice whereby at
-- a trial it is obligatory for the court to warn itself about convicting the accused on the uncorroborated testimony of an
-- accomplice is abrogated.  The court may still treat an accomplice's evidence with caution under section 116 illustration (b).
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 135(1)"
GIVEN `the person is an accomplice of the accused` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the accomplice is a competent witness against the accused` IF
    `the person is an accomplice of the accused`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 135(2)"
`the court is obliged to warn itself about convicting on the uncorroborated testimony of an accomplice` MEANS FALSE

-- 136: no particular number of witnesses is in any case required for the proof of any fact.
@ref "Evidence Act 1893, s 136"
`a particular number of witnesses is required for the proof of a fact` MEANS FALSE
